// @emit-error cannot be discriminated
// The numeric twin of `same-field-names-literal-tags-reject.vl`, and it needed NO new guard.
//
// Two arms differing only by their tag share a field-NAME set, so `assignTags` compares them
// through the layout-equal-twin exemption, which reads the fields' identity text
// (`uFieldTyText`). A numeric literal renders through canon as its BASE SCALAR for exactly
// the same reason a string one renders as `string`, so both arms would have recorded `i32`,
// compared EQUAL, folded as one variant, and made `is C` answer TRUE for a value built
// through `R` — rc 0, a valid module, the wrong branch.
//
// `variantFieldIdText` records EVERY `TyLit` kind rather than only `"str"`. That was written
// when the string family was admitted, with the numeric one still refused at the checker —
// so the column was already correct for a family that could not yet reach it, and admitting
// the arm needed nothing here. This file is the pin that says so.
//
// As with the string twin, making this pair actually WORK needs the tag to carry a runtime
// identity the arms can be told apart by; VL's structural unions discriminate on the
// field-NAME set (`docs/guide/unions.md`), so until then LOUD is the correct column.
type C = { kind: 0, size: f64 }
type R = { kind: 1, size: f64 }
type S = C | R

const c: S = { kind: 1, size: 2.0 }
if c is C { print("C") } else { print("R") }
